@node CMacs AI Harness
@chapter CMacs AI Harness: an Agent in a Buffer

@cindex AI harness
@cindex agentic harness
@cindex transcript

@kbd{M-x cmacs-ai-harness} opens an agentic AI session in a buffer: a
transcript the library owns, rendered natively, with an editable prompt
at the end.

It is @emph{not} @code{cmacs-ai-chat}, and the difference is worth
stating because the two look similar from outside.  A chat buffer is an
Org document you can edit and save; a harness buffer is a rendered view
of a transcript ai-glib owns, redrawn from its blocks.  A chat is a
conversation you keep; a harness is an agent you are driving.  That is
also why exporting to Org exists here and not there --- the buffer is
not Org to begin with.

@section The three entry points

@table @code
@item cmacs-ai-harness
Asks nothing.  Uses @code{cmacs-ai-harness-provider},
@code{cmacs-ai-harness-model} and @code{default-directory}.
@item cmacs-ai-harness-with-provider
Asks for a provider, then a model.
@item cmacs-ai-harness-with-provider-in-directory
Asks for a provider, a directory, then a model.
@end table

The directory is not cosmetic.  @file{CLAUDE.md}, @file{.claude}, the
project's own command files and every relative path a tool touches
resolve against it --- an agent started in the wrong directory is a
different agent.

@section Keys

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .8
@headitem Key @tab Does
@item @kbd{C-c C-c} @tab send the prompt
@item @kbd{C-c C-k} @tab cancel the run, or clear the prompt when idle
@item @kbd{C-c C-g} @tab write the prompt in a dedicated buffer
@item @kbd{C-c C-e} @tab export the session as Org
@item @kbd{C-c E} @tab export the session as markdown
@item @kbd{C-c C-t} @tab expand or collapse the block at point
@item @kbd{TAB} @tab complete a @code{/command} or an @code{@@path}
@end multitable

@kbd{C-c C-k} is one key for two things because that is what the
terminal harness's @kbd{^C} does, and the two are never ambiguous:
there is either a run to stop or there is not.

@subsection The compose buffer

@kbd{C-c C-g} opens a compose buffer seeded with whatever is already in
the prompt, so reaching for a real buffer part-way through a long prompt
does not lose it.  @kbd{C-c C-c} there installs the text as the prompt
and closes the buffer; @kbd{C-c C-k} discards it.

The terminal harness hands the prompt to @env{$EDITOR} for this.  Inside
Emacs that would be an editor inside an editor, so it is just a buffer.

@section Export

@kbd{C-c C-e} (Org) and @kbd{C-c E} (markdown) prompt with
@code{read-file-name}, so it
is the same directory browsing and completion as @kbd{C-x C-f} rather
than a bespoke picker.  The default name mirrors
@code{cmacs-ai-chat-save-name-format} with @samp{harness} in it:

@example
260816-225824-harness-ollama.org
@end example

controlled by @code{cmacs-ai-harness-export-name-format}, under
@code{cmacs-ai-harness-dir}.  The extension comes from ai-glib, so an
Org export and @command{ai-tui}'s @code{/export org} cannot disagree
about what to call it.

Three things about the export are ai-glib's doing and worth knowing:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Blocks export expanded whatever the buffer shows.}  A collapsed
tool group renders as one summary line, and a file recording ``Edited 3
files'' without saying which is not a record of anything.
@item
@strong{The rendering is not the content.}  The @samp{>} markers and the
@samp{✳ thinking ⌄} chrome orient a reader on screen and are noise in a
file --- in Org, @samp{>} is not even syntax.  Prose blocks export their
own text; tool and todo blocks keep their markers, because there
@samp{✓} and @samp{+21-6} @emph{are} the information.
@item
@strong{Prose passes through, output is fenced.}  Models already write
markdown, so escaping an answer would ruin every code fence in it.
@end itemize

@section The AI menu

Right-click (or @code{cmacs-ai-menu-pick}) offers a @strong{Harness}
group in these buffers: write this prompt for me, summarize this
session, next steps from this session, explain this block, copy this
block, and export as markdown or Org.

``Write this prompt for me'' does two different things on purpose.  With
an empty prompt it asks what you want and drafts one.  With a draft
already there it strengthens what you wrote without broadening the
scope --- replacing your intent with its own would make the feature
worse than typing it yourself.

``Summarize this session'' reads the @emph{exported} transcript rather
than the buffer text, so it can say what the agent actually did and not
only what it narrated.

@section How it works

The grouping, the summarising and the input pipeline all live in
ai-glib.  This buffer knows nothing about what a tool is, exactly as
@command{ai-tui} does not.

@subsection Why a C bridge and not cmacs-gi

ai-glib's own documentation shows this frontend written in Elisp over
GObject Introspection.  That does not work here yet: cmacs-gi skips
out-parameters entirely and has no signal support, and the harness needs
both.  @code{ai_rendered_text_get_span()} and
@code{ai_completion_result_get_item_fields()} are out-parameter APIs
@emph{because} a plain struct behind a pointer does not survive
@command{g-ir-scanner}, and @code{::block-changed} is what makes
streaming appear at all.

Teaching cmacs-gi those two things is worth doing and is a bigger,
riskier change to a subsystem everything else already depends on.  So
@file{cmacs/ai/cmacs-ai-harness.c} is a purpose-built bridge, in the
shape @file{cmacs-ai-stream.c} already uses.

@subsection Two things a frontend gets wrong

Both are handled in one place here, and both are silent when wrong:

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Span offsets are byte offsets into UTF-8.}
@code{byte-to-position} converts.  Skip it and every face after the
first accented filename lands somewhere else.
@item
@strong{@code{::block-changed} is not optional.}  Streaming mutates a
block in place, which the insertion signal does not report.  Without it
the buffer shows the first delta of each reply and then stops.
@end enumerate

@subsection Evil, and why the mode is not a special-mode

The obvious base class for a buffer with a read-only transcript is
@code{special-mode}, and it is the wrong one.  Two things follow from it
and both are wrong here: the buffer becomes read-only outright, and
@strong{Evil gives modes derived from @code{special-mode} normal state}
--- so under Doom every letter you type at the prompt is a motion or an
operator, and the buffer looks broken rather than modal.

So the mode derives from @code{fundamental-mode}, the transcript is
protected by a @code{read-only} text property covering exactly the part
that needs it, and the buffer opens in @emph{insert} state --- the same
treatment eshell, vterm and a commit message get, because the thing you
do here is type.  @kbd{ESC} still reaches normal state for reading back
through the transcript.

The @code{evil-set-initial-state} call is inside
@code{with-eval-after-load}, not behind a bare @code{fboundp} guard as
some other cmacs modes use: that guard silently does nothing when the
file loads before Evil, and the failure mode is landing in normal state,
which does not look like an Evil problem when you hit it.

@subsection Block ids, not positions

@code{cmacs-ai-harness-block-at} reports an @code{:id} unique for the
life of the process.  The buffer's region map is keyed on it, because
positions shift when blocks are removed and ids do not.

@section Configuration

@multitable @columnfractions .4 .6
@headitem Variable @tab Means
@item @code{cmacs-ai-harness-provider} @tab provider symbol, or the configured default
@item @code{cmacs-ai-harness-model} @tab model, or the provider's own default
@item @code{cmacs-ai-harness-dir} @tab where exports are offered first
@item @code{cmacs-ai-harness-export-name-format} @tab export file name, without extension
@item @code{cmacs-ai-harness-prompt} @tab the prompt marker
@end multitable

Faces are one per ai-glib style role.  The library's tags are
@strong{roles, not colours}: @code{added} says ``this run is an
addition'' and says nothing about green, so the mapping is cmacs's and a
theme can override any of it.
